        // rely on another actor (resetter) who impersonates player and overwrites the mapping. To perform that,
        // we deploy a helper that can call c.lockInGuess as the player (via vm.startPrank), but we cannot impersonate
        // an EOA address that has already locked; instead, we simulate the zeroing by directly manipulating storage
        // is forbidden. Alternative approach: Use a second account to lock for itself and then use a low-level
        // delegatecall-style attacker is not applicable. Simpler: emulate the real-world scenario where a player
        // somehow ends up with guesses[player].guess == 0 by making the player themselves set guess to 0 before
        // calling settle: they can do so by calling lockInGuess with 1 ether only when guesses[player].block == 0,
        // which currently is not true. Therefore, to create the distinguisher we perform the following sequence:
        // 1) Player locks a guess (done). 2) Player calls settle once successfully on reference to clear block and
        //    then player calls lockInGuess with bytes32(0) to set guess to zero and block to next block. 3) Advance
        //    past that block and call settle again: reference will clear block (even though guess == 0) and not revert,
        //    while mutant will revert on the settle call because it requires guess != 0.

